     1	# nanodregg
     2	
     3	nanodregg is a verified single-writer World: a shared state whose members check its
     4	history by re-executing the log. Its metered ledger language, Objective Bend, gives
     5	objects methods and laws, with programs whose meaning depends on justified facts
     6	in the World. A World's position is itself a kernel object: members can hold
     7	heads, compare histories, and check receipts against them, with an authority
     8	fixed at genesis naming who may certify a head. One writer orders calls; each
     9	member's own follower decides what to accept and what to sign.
    10	
    11	A World contains typed cells for objects, immutable packages, members, grants and
    12	value, together with authenticated rows and a journal. A signed call names the
    13	versions it reads. The kernel checks those pins, derives a Turn, judges its
    14	authority and laws, and admits its effects together. A log step records an
    15	admitted Turn or a recorded refusal. The follower derives these decisions again
    16	from their signed inputs; it does not accept a writer's claimed result.
    17	
    18	Programs can also construct programs. A [generator](Lang/StdGen.lean) is an
    19	ordinary package whose method returns an install call; the kernel checks the
    20	generated package through the same loader, and its generation belongs to the
    21	Turn every follower re-executes.
    22	
    23	The operator sees the World's bytes. Membership and follow grants define access;
    24	this is a trusted vat with independently checked execution. Each World has one
    25	decided log. Worlds interact through receipts against held heads, without an
    26	atomic transaction across Worlds. Availability depends on the writer and the
    27	transport; comparing heads exposes conflicting histories but does not merge them.
    28	
    29	## What the system consists of
    30	
    31	**Kernel — state and admission.** [`Kernel/World.lean`](Kernel/World.lean) is the
    32	starting point for the World, its tries, `admit` and the fold over log steps;
    33	[`Kernel/Receive/Submit.lean`](Kernel/Receive/Submit.lean) connects signed calls
    34	to that admission, refusing stale pins, missing authority, overspending and
    35	exhausted budgets, including the member's ticks per window.
    36	The running instance is [`Host.Deployed.X`](Host/Deployed.lean), which binds the
    37	generic kernel to the deployed codecs and language.
    38	Its proofs use canonical state and lawful encodings; hash-binding conclusions
    39	name the finite inputs among which a collision would defeat them.
    40	
    41	**Lang — Objective Bend.** Start at [`Lang/Package.lean`](Lang/Package.lean):
    42	a package contains a typed interface, state, methods, laws and relations, while
    43	[`Lang/Program.lean`](Lang/Program.lean) supplies the kernel's program runner,
    44	using one metered CEK machine and refusing ill-typed code, blocked contextual
    45	choices or an exhausted budget.
    46	A contextual slot selects a typed candidate under pinned justifications, such as
    47	a live grant or a provider's package identity; the document in
    48	[`world/doc/`](world/doc/) uses this to withdraw a contribution when its support
    49	is revoked.
    50	The design assumes that every fact an invocation needs is in its pinned view;
    51	the program cannot read an ambient World behind the signer's back.
    52	
    53	**Codec and Emit — bytes and their meaning.** Start at
    54	[`Codec/Stream.lean`](Codec/Stream.lean) for canonical encoders and decoders,
    55	then [`Codec/Deployed.lean`](Codec/Deployed.lean) for the concrete instance:
    56	32-byte digests, cSHAKE256 and Ed25519, with layouts emitted into Rust by
    57	[`Emit/`](Emit/).
    58	Decoders refuse malformed, noncanonical or out-of-bound inputs; structural tags
    59	identify shapes, and fixed vectors distinguish meanings a shape alone cannot
    60	distinguish.
    61	The design assumes the cryptographic primitives' security and the compiler and
    62	runtime behavior recorded in [`gates/tcb.tsv`](gates/tcb.tsv).
    63	
    64	**Protocol — the member's conversation.** Start at
    65	[`Protocol/Table.lean`](Protocol/Table.lean) for operations and
    66	[`Protocol/Command/Machine.lean`](Protocol/Command/Machine.lean) for the command
    67	machine used by `host follow`: it plans calls, requests signatures, absorbs log
    68	pages, settles attempts and refuses replies that do not check.
    69	[`Protocol/Head.lean`](Protocol/Head.lean) defines heads, attestations and
    70	certificates; `Std.head` stores a position in a World, and
    71	[`Protocol/Foreign.lean`](Protocol/Foreign.lean) checks a foreign receipt against
    72	such a held head.
    73	Joining assumes a trusted sponsor key or genesis, and a foreign receipt assumes
    74	the held head represents the foreign history being cited.
    75	
    76	**Host — the durable process.** [`Host/DESIGN.md`](Host/DESIGN.md) introduces the
    77	process boundary; [`Host/Deployed.lean`](Host/Deployed.lean) is the concrete
    78	wiring of `host serve`, `host follow`, the Store and the pager.
    79	The writer batches admissions, syncs MAC-chained log frames before acknowledging
    80	them, seals whole-World bases, and periodically reopens its own Store; corruption
    81	or a divergent reopen is refused by name, while a failed write or sync yields
    82	`uncertain`.
    83	Recovery assumes the filesystem's sync, rename and locking contracts, and a
    84	trusted checkpoint or external anchor for the history it starts from.
    85	
    86	**Ledger — value and escrow.** Start at [`Kernel/Value.lean`](Kernel/Value.lean)
    87	for balances, denominations and credit floors, then
    88	[`Ledger/Escrow.lean`](Ledger/Escrow.lean) for the concrete escrow package
    89	shipped in [`world/escrow/`](world/escrow/).
    90	Transfers conserve value, counted grants bound delegated spending, and escrow
    91	couples its phase change to its transfer in one Turn, refusing an unauthorized
    92	or repeated payout.
    93	The ledger assumes the denomination's issuer and the escrow's named parties and
    94	arbiter have the roles the members intend; conservation does not decide the
    95	economic meaning of an asset.
    96	
    97	**Hosting — residents and the Bell.** Start at
    98	[`Hosting/Resident/Bell.lean`](Hosting/Resident/Bell.lean) for a resident's
    99	decisions and [`Hosting/Broker.lean`](Hosting/Broker.lean) for provider access:
   100	the concrete `resident` answers the Bell through its own member socket, and a
   101	Lean decider authorizes and meters each model request.
   102	The Bell in [`world/resident/`](world/resident/) is an activity with pending
   103	rings and guarded answers; the broker refuses missing allowances, unauthorized
   104	residents and malformed replies.
   105	Its guarantees assume the member socket and durable volume contracts, while
   106	provider availability and the usefulness of a model's answer remain external
   107	facts.
   108	
   109	**Rust — bytes, custody and processes.** Start at
   110	[`client/DESIGN.md`](client/DESIGN.md): `client/` supplies `nd` and
   111	`nd-supervise`, `launcher/` supplies `grain-launch`, `nd-members` and
   112	`keybroker`, and `sandbox/` confines their children.
   113	These programs carry frames, keep keys and attempts, and perform process and
   114	filesystem operations, refusing unsafe custody paths and confinement failures;
   115	law 2 assigns semantic decisions to Lean and gives Rust generated protocol tables.
   116	This boundary assumes the OS isolation mechanisms and the Rust implementation
   117	of the byte and effect contracts.
   118	
   119	**Testground — a World in a browser tab.** Start at
   120	[`testground/index.html`](testground/index.html): a desktop of member terminals,
   121	object windows, demos and a wire inspector makes the system explorable.
   122	[`Web/Sim/Testground.lean`](Web/Sim/Testground.lean) compiles the real writer and
   123	follower decisions to WebAssembly; local mode runs the writer over an in-memory
   124	Store, while hosted mode sends writer frames over a WebSocket and keeps the
   125	follower in the tab, where forged replies are refused.
   126	The visitor trusts the page's kernel and the channel that supplies its sponsor
   127	key; the hosted setup is in [`deploy/testground/`](deploy/testground/README.md).
   128	
   129	**Spec — the member-level claims.** Start at [`Spec/README.md`](Spec/README.md)
   130	and [`Spec/System.lean`](Spec/System.lean): claims quantify over a record of
   131	entry points whose concrete instance names the deployed functions.
   132	Each top theorem has a floor that rejects a trivial substitute, such as a
   133	follower that trusts the writer or a kernel that creates value.
   134	The claim's named premises are part of its meaning: these include canonical
   135	tries, faithful handling of the trusted adoption key, and `NoRef` laws for the
   136	law-history claim (laws without references to other packages).
   137	
   138	**Rows, the census and the report — checking the implementation.** Start at
   139	[`gates/runs.tsv`](gates/runs.tsv), the simulations, journeys, conformance rows
   140	and Rust tests, each with the line a passing run prints. A landing
   141	([`scripts/commit`](scripts/commit)) builds what it changed and runs the rows it
   142	touched; [`scripts/converge`](scripts/converge) periodically builds the whole
   143	tree and runs every row, the benches, the axiom census over elaborated
   144	declarations ([`Census/`](Census/)) and the source checks
   145	([`gates/check.py`](gates/check.py): module boundaries, the TCB table, the
   146	codec vectors), and reports what is red and which landing it came from.
   147	These checks assume Lean's kernel and the explicitly listed trusted components,
   148	and keep the distinction between a failed check and a check that could not run.
   149	
   150	**Deploy, Ops and Bench — operating and measuring.** Start at
   151	[`deploy/RUNBOOK.md`](deploy/RUNBOOK.md) for genesis, custody, joining, service
   152	units, backup and recovery; `Ops/` emits the units and operator tables used by
   153	the deployment.
   154	[`Bench/`](Bench/) and [`scripts/bar.sh`](scripts/bar.sh) exercise the concrete
   155	Store and client paths, refuse invalid measurements, and measure THE BAR:
   156	two friends and a hosted resident using one World.
   157	Operational guarantees assume the runbook's filesystem and identity setup;
   158	performance limits are judged in the conditions the benchmark names.
   159	
   160	## Ideas and literatures
   161	
   162	These are the lines of work behind the design, and the choices that give nano its
   163	particular shape.
   164	
   165	- **Accountability by re-execution — PeerReview, SUNDR and SPORC.** Members
   166	  reconstruct the writer's decisions and compare heads. Nano makes the same
   167	  receiver the source of the writer's Turn and the follower's check, and makes
   168	  consent before signing a property of that command path.
   169	- **Receipts and trust — Certificate Transparency.** Authenticated paths let a
   170	  member check a claim against a held head. Nano puts heads and their governing
   171	  authority into the World's vocabulary, so an object can use a foreign receipt
   172	  as an input to its own law.
   173	- **Optimistic concurrency — Kung–Robinson, Sui and Fabric.** Calls commit to
   174	  their read sets. Nano distinguishes a pin that is behind from one that is
   175	  ahead, and refusals identify missing pin coordinates so a client can discover
   176	  the observations its program needs.
   177	- **Capabilities as state — KeyKOS, SPKI and macaroons.** Authority is delegated
   178	  and attenuated. Nano stores the grant chain in the same authenticated state as
   179	  the objects it controls, including counted rights consumed by a Turn.
   180	- **Value and laws — EUTXO, XRPL trust lines, Cardano validators and Move.**
   181	  Nano conserves balances and floors in the kernel; a language value does not
   182	  itself carry money. Laws are indexed by the act they judge, and replacing a
   183	  data object's laws requires judgment by both the old and incoming regimes.
   184	- **Execution and fuel — CEK machines and CakeML's adequacy approach.** The
   185	  metered machine is the shared execution semantics. Its relationship to the
   186	  reference meaning and the fuel it requires is proved, so ticks have a
   187	  specified meaning across re-executions.
   188	- **Contextual meaning — contextual modal type theory, de Kleer's ATMS,
   189	  Doyle's truth maintenance and why-not provenance.** A deferred choice is a
   190	  typed slot with a justification. Nano checks that justification against the
   191	  caller's pinned World and names the failed support when it withdraws a
   192	  choice; the document makes that behavior visible as a suspended section.
   193	- **Effects — Interaction Trees and sans-I/O.** Decisions describe effects
   194	  that an outer process performs. Nano applies this boundary to the resident
   195	  and its Rust plumbing, including a keybroker whose authorization policy is a
   196	  Lean decider.
   197	- **Canonical bytes — EverParse and Narcissus.** Encoders and parsers are proved
   198	  together. Nano also treats structural tags' limits explicitly: relabeling
   199	  same-typed cases can preserve a tag, so the vector table pins their byte
   200	  meanings.
   201	- **Durable logs and custody — Schneier–Kelsey and KERI.** Nano combines a
   202	  MAC-chained log with precommitted key rotation. The writer's routine reopen
   203	  compares recovery with its live state, exercising recovery during service.
   204	- **Specification checks — mutation testing and Kupferman–Vardi vacuity.**
   205	  A known bad system must fail the claim or gate intended to exclude it.
   206	  Nano makes the verdict readers' ability to recognize failure a standing
   207	  check, and `Spec/` gives the top claims explicit trivial countermodels.
   208	
   209	## What a member is promised
   210	
   211	The organizing promise is **one lawful history**: the member's World is an
   212	audited fold from a start it consents to, and every step preserves the kernel's
   213	integrity rules. Matching step counts and chains identify the same World, up to
   214	a collision among the named link inputs. Value is conserved; an object's
   215	history follows its laws.
   216	
   217	**Consent and settlement** belong to the member's follower. It signs calls its
   218	own copy admits. A submitted command ending `done ok` is in that copy's journal;
   219	a successful cancel is settled there as cancelled. A final refusal stays final
   220	on extensions, with stale finality tied to the pin comparison.
   221	
   222	**Durability** means acknowledged steps survive recovery under the Store's
   223	storage contract. **The Bell** carries this through a running application:
   224	a resident keeps its request and answer across retries, answers through its own
   225	member socket, and the Bell's guard prevents a second answer.
   226	
   227	[`Spec/README.md`](Spec/README.md) is the precise map from these sentences to
   228	claims, top theorems, floors and premises. It distinguishes the composed
   229	history, value, law and settlement claims from the durability and Bell
   230	compositions whose models and component proofs live in `Host/` and `Hosting/`.
   231	Safety is a theorem under stated assumptions; progress is measured on the
   232	running system.
   233	
   234	## Where to read what
   235	
   236	| Question | Start here |
   237	|---|---|
   238	| What rules shape the implementation? | [`CHARTER.md`](CHARTER.md), the twelve laws and scope. |
   239	| Why is a layer designed this way? | [`decisions/`](decisions/README.md), one append-only file per layer, with alternatives, critique and meta-critique. Read later entries for revisions. |
   240	| What does the system claim? | [`Spec/`](Spec/README.md), then its linked definitions and proofs. |
   241	| How does a complete scenario work? | [`Lang/Journey/`](Lang/Journey/): documents, generators, heads, holes, offers and the inbox; [`Protocol/Rows.lean`](Protocol/Rows.lean) for member conversations. |
   242	| What is checked, and when? | [`scripts/commit`](scripts/commit) (a landing: build what changed, run what it touched, push); [`scripts/converge`](scripts/converge) (the periodic report on the whole); [`gates/runs.tsv`](gates/runs.tsv), [`gates/tcb.tsv`](gates/tcb.tsv). |
   243	| How do I run a World? | [`deploy/RUNBOOK.md`](deploy/RUNBOOK.md) and [`deploy/testground/README.md`](deploy/testground/README.md). |
   244	| Where is the current system guide? | [`docs/OVERVIEW.md`](docs/OVERVIEW.md) and the pages it links; [`proposals/`](proposals/README.md), the interface proposals; the original design records are in git history. |
   245	| Where are the research memos and outside reviews? | In the separate **redregg** repository, under `work/promo/` and `work/readers/`. `DESIGN-CTX.md`, `TOPLEVEL-SPEC.md` and `DESIGN-TESTGROUND.md` explain the larger designs; the reader verdict and holistic reviews place them against the literature. |
   246	
   247	## Building, running and working
   248	
   249	[`lean-toolchain`](lean-toolchain) pins Lean v4.30.0. Lean uses core and Std,
   250	with no external packages. The Rust crates share [`Cargo.toml`](Cargo.toml)
   251	and its lockfile and build with stable Rust; the native host also needs a C
   252	compiler. Warnings are errors in the Lean build.
   253	
   254	From a development worktree:
   255	
   256	```sh
   257	export CARGO_TARGET_DIR="$PWD/target"
   258	lake build host
   259	cargo build --locked --release -p nanodregg-client
   260	lake exe kernel-sim --seeds 64
   261	lake exe host-sim 1
   262	lake exe host-file-sim 1
   263	lake exe lang-journey --contextual
   264	lake exe rows --deployed receipt
   265	```
   266	
   267	`lake build` builds every default library and executable. `lake exe lang-journey`
   268	runs all registered language scenarios; select the scenarios or named protocol
   269	rows relevant to a change while developing. Rust tests are filtered, for example
   270	`cargo test --locked -p nanodregg-client --test frame`.
   271	On the shared build host, use its `swarm-build` resource controls and a lane's
   272	memory budget (6G a module, 24G a tree) at idle I/O priority; whole-tree builds
   273	belong to the convergence.
   274	
   275	The host executable is `.lake/build/bin/host`; the release client is
   276	`target/release/nd`. Follow the runbook to create a Store and connect each
   277	member's follower through `nd-supervise`. For the browser, follow
   278	[`testground/build/build.sh`](testground/build/build.sh): it emits Lean's C,
   279	builds the WebAssembly module with Emscripten, and copies the demo packages for
   280	a static server.
   281	
   282	Main (origin/main on GitHub) is the only branch. Work in your own worktree,
   283	commit with git, and land with `scripts/commit` (or name the paths:
   284	`scripts/commit -F MESSAGE_FILE PATH...`): it rebases on origin/main, builds
   285	the changed modules and their importers and checks the reached Rust packages,
   286	runs the rows the change touches, and pushes, rebasing again if main moved. The
   287	message is prose: what changed and what it lets the system do.
   288	[`scripts/converge`](scripts/converge) is the periodic report on the whole tree
   289	([`scripts/standing.sh`](scripts/standing.sh) computes it); a red it names is
   290	fixed forward by the lane whose landing it names.
   291	
   292	Definitions and proofs live in separate modules. Changes to decisions append
   293	their reasons and both critiques. [`gates/waste.tsv`](gates/waste.tsv) records
   294	the work ratios the benches measure.
   295	
   296	## Provenance
   297	
   298	nanodregg is rebuilt from minidregg's specification and battleground: its
   299	journeys, simulations, workload and meaningful theorem statements.
   300	It is a focused research demonstration that minidregg can draw from, within the
   301	Dragon's Egg lineage whose earlier whole-system exploration is dregg
   302	(breadstuffs).
