 succeeded in 1572ms:
# The system

Nanodregg is a shared World with one writer and members who check its history themselves. A member signs a call planned against its own view. The writer derives and judges the call, records an admitted turn or a recordable refusal, and serves the history. Followers re-derive each event before accepting the resulting World.

| Layer | Contract |
|---|---|
| [Kernel](../Kernel/World.lean) | Cells, accounts, grants, relations, authenticated roots, budgets and the transition for turns and recorded refusals. [Receive](../Kernel/Receive/Rederive.lean) connects signed calls to those transitions. |
| [Lang](../Lang/Program.lean) | Checked packages supply methods and laws. Imports and external reads use the caller's pinned view; evaluation spends a bounded tick allowance. |
| [Codec](../Codec/Stream.lean) | Canonical encodings, bounded decoders, digests and signatures give the other layers their bytes. |
| [Protocol](../Protocol/Command/Machine.lean) | Commands catch up, plan, request signatures and settle against the follower's own World. Invitations, receipts and heads describe what another party can check. |
| [Host](../Host/Store.lean) | One locked Store persists authenticated records and atomic bases. Bounded pages serve a base or log; a follower persists its own checked history. |
| [Client](../client/src/bin/nd.rs) | Rust holds member secrets, freezes signed attempts, transports frames and performs the steps Lean requests. |
| [Hosting](../Hosting/Resident/Run.lean) | World objects describe resident grains. Confined processes use member and broker sockets; the keybroker holds provider credentials. |
| [Ops and judge](JUDGE.md) | Rendered units operate the processes. A landing builds what it changed and runs what it touched; the periodic convergence report checks the whole. |

Objects run installed packages. Laws judge changes; relations hold bounded keyed rows. Activities, offers, escrow and generators are package constructions over these mechanisms. [OBJECTS](OBJECTS.md) connects them to executable journeys.

An accepted command is more than a writer reply: settlement is checked against the member's own journal and refusal roots. A thin joiner begins with a sponsor-pinned base; [TRUST](TRUST.md) explains that boundary, certificates and foreign receipts.

[Spec](../Spec/README.md) names the composed theorem claims, their premises and their floors. They do not imply secrecy from the operator, multiwriter consensus or an unconditional end-to-end durability or at-most-once guarantee.

Use [MEMBER](MEMBER.md) for commands, [OPERATING](OPERATING.md) for processes and recovery, and [TESTGROUND](TESTGROUND.md) for the browser system.
# Landing and checking changes

A landing rebases on origin/main, builds the changed modules and their dependents, runs the affected tests and journeys, then pushes to main. Documentation-only changes require no build. The commit message explains what changed and what the system can do because of it.

`scripts/commit` does this from the lane's own worktree: commit with git, then run it (or name paths: `scripts/commit -F MESSAGE PATH...`). It replays the commit onto origin/main, builds what the change touched and runs the rows it reaches in `~/nd-land/<worktree>/` on the home disk (the NVMe on the build host), at idle priority under swarm-build, and pushes. A conflict stops it and names the paths; resolve it against both changes and run it again. If main moves before the push it rebases and pushes again, rebuilding only when main changed one of the same paths. A build killed by its memory cap reads NOT BUILT, never red.

Whole-system validation is a periodic report through scripts/converge or its successor. It builds the tree, runs the rows and tests, and attributes failures to the changes that introduced them. A reported failure becomes work for that change's owner to fix forward.

Nothing else judges a landing: there are no judge slots, no queue, no message-form lines. The census, the codec vector table, the TCB table and the waste ceilings are read by the convergence report. The source checks it prints (gates/check.py) cover module closure, refusal names, the 1 MB file law, paths, the runs table, the TCB table, the codec vectors and the waste ratchet.

Keep design decisions in the layer's decision ledger, with critique and meta-critique. Write an entry for a real design choice rather than for every commit.

Use the designated build host and swarm-build resource budget. Keep tests filtered to the affected behavior. Stop only a process identified by its own PID file; never kill by a name pattern. Do not stash shared work or delete another lane's files or caches.

[Spec](../Spec/README.md) defines the composed assurance claims and their premises. A passing build or convergence report does not establish a theorem's meaning or non-vacuity.
# Spec — what nanodregg is for, as theorems

The top of the tree. Each claim below is a `Prop` over `System` (`Spec/System.lean`: the deployed
entry points as fields). It has a top theorem `top_* : Claim (deployed d)`, a floor
`floor_* : ¬ Claim trivial` refuting a lazy system, and a deployed run that reaches it. Only these
claims owe a vacuity argument; a lemma under a top theorem means what the claim says it means
(TOPLEVEL-SPEC §1.3). Design: `redregg/work/promo/TOPLEVEL-SPEC.md`; decisions: `decisions/spec.md`.

**The trusted reading** is `System.lean` + `Trivial.lean` + `Proofs/Claims.lean` + `Proofs/Floor.lean` (≈ 620 lines).
`Proofs/Bridge.lean`, `Proofs/Member.lean` and `Proofs/Deployed.lean` are proofs: checked, not read.

## The claims

| § | claim | one sentence | top theorem | floor (the system it refuses) |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 2 A1 | `OneHistory.member` | whatever frames follow is fed, the member's World is an audited fold from a start it consented to | `top_oneHistory_member` (under `keyFaithful`; `Proofs/Member.lean`) | `floor_oneHistory_member`: the follower that takes the World it is told |
| 2 A2 | `OneHistory.pair` | two folds from one World that agree on step count and chain are one World, up to a collision among their link inputs | `top_oneHistory_pair` | `floor_oneHistory_pair`: the kernel whose chain does not move |
| 3 B2 | `Integrity.value` | along any fold, every denomination's balance sum is unchanged, and its floor sum unless the log created its well | `top_integrity_value` | `floor_integrity_value`: the kernel that admits without `checkValue` |
| 3 B3 | `Integrity.law` | along an audited log an object's package never changes and its states are a chain its `NoRef` laws' reference meaning admits | `top_integrity_law` | `floor_integrity_law`: the receiver whose `write` reaches the package slot |
| 4 C(ii) | `Consent.okCommitted` | a command that ends `done ok` at the settle phase means follow's own World journals the call | `top_consent_okCommitted` | `floor_consent_okCommitted`: the follower that says ok on the Host's word |
| 4 C(iii) | `Consent.final` | a settlement `expired`, or `refused` other than `stale`, is never contradicted on any extension | `top_consent_final` | `floor_consent_final`: the settlement that is always final |
| 4 C(ii) | `Consent.cancelOk` | a `cancel CALL` step that ends `done ok` leaves follow at a World that settles the call `refused cancelled` | `top_consent_cancelOk` | `floor_consent_cancelOk`: the follower that says ok on the writer's word |
| 4 C(iii) | `Consent.staleFinal` | a planned call follow settles `refused stale` is admitted on no extension, up to a collision among its pin inputs | `top_consent_staleFinal` | `floor_consent_staleFinal`: the settlement that says stale on its own word |
| 8 G2 | `Shown.onlyFollowers` | the writer defers a frame (answers with a page) only past an `Envelope.check` on its World | `top_shown` | `floor_shown`: the host that serves every frame |

**The apex** is `OneHistory.member ∧ Integrity.*`: the member's World is audited (A1) and audited
means lawful (B2, B3). Neither half excludes the other's trivial system: a kernel that admits
everything makes every log `Decided`, which A1 cannot see and B2/B3 refuse.

## Premises (each a parameter of the claim, named; deleting one is a strengthening)

* **`keyFaithful`** (A1): every continuation that parses as `adopt INVITE KEY` names the sponsor key
  the member trusts. Under an arbitrary relay A1 is FALSE today: a forged continuation makes a fresh
  member adopt any base the forger pinned (SPEC-4). Deleted by keeping the command's state inside
  follow (TOPLEVEL-SPEC §11.2).
* **`NoRef`** (B3): the chain lift exists for laws without holes only (TOPLEVEL-SPEC §11.6).
* **Canonical tries** (B2: `w.Canon`; B3: `CellsCanon`; C(iii): `RefusalsCanon`, `JournalCanon`): true of
  every World a Store opens to (`Kept`), carried as premises because the claims are generic in `D`.

B2's floor starts at the genesis World and ends at a World no deployed fold reaches, reached by the
minting kernel's fold (SPEC-7). B3's floor refutes the package clause; the law-chain clause has no
floor of its own yet (SPEC-7).

## Inhabited

At `deployed storeDigest` (the digest every deployed Store pins by; it does not depend on the Store's
key): `inhabited_okCommitted` (follow, holding the World THE re-deriver reaches on the run's log,
ends the rotate's settle phase `done ok`: C(ii)'s premise) and `inhabited_final` (on that World
follow settles `expired` a call the writer would have admitted at genesis: C(iii)'s premise, by a
call that was not final before), `inhabited_shown` (the writer defers the founder's `log` frame under
its root grant's envelope at the genesis World: G2's premise) and `inhabited_value` (the founder
issues a well, opens an account and transfers 10 into it; THE re-deriver accepts the three calls and
the account holds 10: B2's non-trivial case, value moved).

`deployed_run` / `inhabited_audited` (`Floor.lean`): at the deployed `Ctx`, a genesis World with one
founder and the founder's signed `rotate`, re-derived by THE re-deriver, reaches height 1 — a
`native_decide` fact about a computed run (SPEC-5). It inhabits A1/A2/B2/B3's premise (an audited log
with a Turn); it does not move value or run a law, so the non-trivial cases of B2 and B3 are not yet
reached by a deployed run (B2's is, by `inhabited_value`; B3's — a law judging a step — is not);
nor is a cancel that ends `ok`.

## Not yet claims (TOPLEVEL-SPEC sections, and why)

* B1 authority (§3.1): the `Authorized` inductive over every cell kind; the statement is the work.
* B2's grant cap and escrow (§3.2): exist as theorems (`grant_moves_le_cap`, `escrow_invokes_one_payout`).
* A2′ fork consistency (§2.3): landed as an act (PROTOCOL-74/75); the claim over `Command.step` is new.
* C(i) signing, C(v) at most once (§4).
* DURABILITY (§5), THE BELL (§6), RECEIPTS BETWEEN WORLDS (§9): their models (`Crash`, `RingLife`)
  or the writer's signed heads are §11's items.
/-
# Spec.System — the deployed entry points, as one record

The top-level claims (`Spec.Proofs.Claims`) are predicates on a `System`: every component a claim
ranges over is a field, so a trivial system (one component replaced by what a lazy implementation
would do) can be plugged in, and the claim's floor is a theorem that it refuses that system
(`Spec.Proofs.Floor`). `deployed` fills every field with the definition the binaries run.

The fields are the decisions, not their callers: the kernel's derivation of a signed call
(`receive`), its re-judge of a logged refusal (`recordedBy`), the admission of a Turn and the
record of a refusal (`admit`, `refuse`), the writer's verdict on a submitted call (`submit`),
one frame of the member's command machine (`follow`, run by `host follow`), the member's
settlement of its own call (`settle`), and the writer's answer to a frame (`handle`). What a field
does not vary (the call codec, the signature check, the Lang program, the hash) is `X`.
-/
import Kernel.Receive.Rederive
import Kernel.Receive.Follow
import Protocol.Command.Machine
import Host.Serve
import Host.Deployed
import Host.Keys

namespace Nanodregg.Spec

open Nanodregg.Kernel Nanodregg.Kernel.Receive Nanodregg.Protocol Nanodregg.Codec

structure System (D : Type) [Ord D] [DecidableEq D] where
  /-- the context every field reads through: encodings, hash, call codec, `verify`, the program -/
  X : Ctx D
  /-- the digest codec a receipt is written in -/
  dc : Codec.Stream D
  /-- how follow reads an adopted base: its World codec, step count, pin digest, size bound -/
  B : Thin.Base (World D)
  /-- the kernel's derivation of a signed call into a Turn (`Receive.receive`) -/
  receive : World D → Call D → Except (Refusal D) (Turn D)
  /-- the kernel's re-judge of a logged refusal (`Receive.recordedBy`) -/
  recordedBy : World D → Call D → Refused D → Outcome D
  /-- admission of a Turn (`World.admit`) -/
  admit : World D → Turn D → Except (Reject D) (World D)
  /-- the record of a refusal (`World.refuse`) -/
  refuse : World D → Refused D → Except (Reject D) (World D)
  /-- the writer's verdict on a submitted call (`Receive.submit`) -/
  submit : World D → Call D → Except (Verdict D) (Turn D × World D)
  /-- one frame of the member's command machine (`Command.step`, what `host follow` answers) -/
  follow : Command.Self D → Command.Follow D → ByteArray → ByteArray →
    Command.Follow D × Command.Persist D × Command.Reply
  /-- the member's settlement of its own call on its own World (`Command.settleOf`) -/
  settle : Command.Self D → World D → Call D → Settle.Verdict D
  /-- the writer's answer to a frame on its World (`Host.handle`) -/
  handle : World D → Host.Frame → Host.Answer (World D) (Step D)

variable {D : Type} [Ord D] [DecidableEq D]

namespace System

/-- One step of a log under the system's kernel (`World.step`'s shape). -/
def step (S : System D) (w : World D) : Step D → Except (Reject D) (World D)
  | .turn t => S.admit w t
  | .refusal r => S.refuse w r

/-- A log under the system's kernel (`World.fold`'s shape): the World it reaches, or the index of
the first step refused. -/
def fold (S : System D) (w : World D) : List (Step D) → Except (Nat × Reject D) (World D)
  | [] => .ok w
  | s :: rest =>
      match S.step w s with
      | .ok w' =>
          match S.fold w' rest with
          | .ok w'' => .ok w''
          | .error (i, r) => .error (i + 1, r)
      | .error r => .error (0, r)

end System

/-- The system the binaries run, over the deployed `Ctx` (`Host.Deployed.X`). `worldDigest` is the
Store's pin digest (`Keys.worldDigest`), which `host follow` reads from its Store's keys; the base
`host follow` hands the command machine is exactly this one (`Host/Follow.lean`, `answer`'s call). -/
def deployed (worldDigest : ByteArray → ByteArray) : System Digest where
  X := Host.Deployed.X
  dc := Codec.Deployed.digestCodec
  B := ⟨Host.Deployed.world, World.steps, worldDigest, Host.Deployed.c.worldBytesMax⟩
  receive := receive Host.Deployed.X
  recordedBy := recordedBy Host.Deployed.X
  admit := World.admit Host.Deployed.X.env
  refuse := World.refuse Host.Deployed.X.env
  submit := submit Host.Deployed.X
  follow := Command.step Host.Deployed.X Codec.Deployed.digestCodec
    ⟨Host.Deployed.world, World.steps, worldDigest, Host.Deployed.c.worldBytesMax⟩
  settle := Command.settleOf Host.Deployed.X
  handle := Host.handle Host.Deployed.X

/-- The digest a Store pins a World's bytes by: `Host.Keys.ofKey`'s `worldDigest`, which does not
depend on the Store's key (an unkeyed digest under `domBaseDigest`, HOST-15). `deployed storeDigest`
is the system every deployed Store runs. -/
def storeDigest (b : ByteArray) : ByteArray := (Codec.Hash.digest Host.domBaseDigest b).bytes

end Nanodregg.Spec

