4:`drainM` is the loop's batch cut, generic in the monad. Over a list model of the port (`poll` pops
5:the front, `unread` pushes a frame back on the front), the batch followed by what the port still
6:holds is exactly the first frame followed by what the port held: every frame the loop took is in
7:the batch or back in the port, in arrival order, and the batch is never empty. Since the loop
8:holds nothing between batches but the port (`serve` takes no carried frame), a round may end
9:`fuel` at any batch boundary and the next round, on the same port, sees every frame not yet
10:batched: no accepted frame is lost across a restart.
11:-/
12:import Host.Loop
13:import Host.Proofs.Batch
