/-
# Host.Follow — `host follow`: the member's own Store, audited at follow time, driven by steps

    loop, one frame at a time (conn, op, payload) from the supervisor:
      command(argv)      → Command.start on follow's World                     → a step
      resume(k, result)  → Command.step: absorbs any log page it carried, every event re-derived
                           (PROTOCOL-4)                                        → a step
      steps absorbed     → appended to follow's own segment as one batch record, durably, BEFORE
                           the step is answered (`Host.append`, the writer's own append path);
                           a page of more steps than one record holds (`recordTurnsMax`, which
                           an honest page never exceeds: `Page.build_events_le`) is refused
                           `recordTooLarge`, never written as a record its reopen refuses

Follow decides nothing the kernel does not: `Command.step` re-judges every logged event (a Turn by
`Receive.submit`, a recorded refusal by `Receive.judge`, which must record the very same refusal)
BEFORE anything is appended, so follow's Store holds only steps its own `World.step` took
(`Protocol.Command.step_persists_admitted`). Its Store is the same format as
the writer's (key, segment of batch records, sealed base) and is reopened EXACTLY as the writer's
is: the one `openWriter`, folding with `World.step` (`Deployed.foldStep`), no signature verified at
reopen (HOST-22, ROOT ruling 2026-10-17: the base and the tail rest under one MAC and key, so a
reopen re-audit distinguishes nothing the MAC does not). A logged event that does not re-derive is refused by the step
(`eventMismatch`), and a page whose stated chain is not follow's fold by `chainMismatch`. Both are
PROTOCOL's names, carried in the step's `done`.

Refusals before the machine, by name: `undecodableCommand`, `undecodableResume`, `wrongSocket`
(submit, lookup, read, log belong to host.sock), `unknownOp`. One refusal AFTER the machine:
`stepTooLarge`, a step whose reply has no wire bytes (a field past `frameBytesMax`, or a `.host`
submit, `Protocol.Command.Wire`). That step's effects are kept: the steps it absorbed (catch-up of
the writer's log, each re-derived) are appended, or the base it adopted is sealed, BEFORE the
refusal is sent; only the reply is refused (HOST-77). The member's next command starts from that
durable World.
-/
import Host.Deployed
import Protocol.Command.Wire

namespace Nanodregg.Host.Follow

open Nanodregg.Kernel Nanodregg.Codec Nanodregg.Host.Deployed Nanodregg.Protocol

/-- Why follow refuses a frame before the machine sees it (law 7). -/
inductive Refusal where
  | undecodableCommand
  | undecodableResume
  | wrongSocket (op : Protocol.Op)
  | unknownOp (code : UInt8)
  | stepTooLarge
  deriving Repr

def Refusal.name : Refusal → String
  | .undecodableCommand => "undecodableCommand"
  | .undecodableResume => "undecodableResume"
  | .wrongSocket _ => "wrongSocket"
/-- `--retain N`: refused `usage` by the name `retainBelowFloor` under the floor of two files
(`Host.retainFloor`), `usage retain` when N is not a number; nothing is opened before it parses. -/
def withRetain (n : String) (k : Retain → IO UInt32) : IO UInt32 :=
  match Retain.parse n with
  | .ok r => k r
  | .error name => refuse .usage name

/-- `host genesis`'s options, in any order before the founders: `--nonce HEX32` (KERNEL-24),
`--certifier HEX32` (repeatable, in order) and `--threshold N` (KERNEL-69; neither given is
`Authority.none`). -/
def genesisOpts (store name : String) : List String → Option String → List String → Option String → IO UInt32
  | "--nonce" :: n :: rest, _, cs, t => genesisOpts store name rest (some n) cs t
  | "--certifier" :: k :: rest, n, cs, t => genesisOpts store name rest n (cs ++ [k]) t
  | "--threshold" :: v :: rest, n, cs, _ => genesisOpts store name rest n cs (some v)
  | fs, n, cs, t => genesisVerb store name n cs t fs

def verb : List String → IO UInt32
  | "genesis" :: store :: name :: rest => genesisOpts store name rest none [] none
  | ["serve", store] => serveVerb store none
  | ["serve", store, "--retain", n] => withRetain n (serveVerb store)
  | ["seal", store] => sealVerb store none
  | ["seal", store, "--retain", n] => withRetain n (sealVerb store)
  | ["follow", store, member, grant] => followVerb store member grant
  | ["join", store, inv, key] => joinVerb store inv key none
  | ["join", store, inv, key, inp] => joinVerb store inv key (some inp)
  | ["check", store] => Check.run store none
  | ["check", store, "--anchor", a] => do
    let some a := Check.Anchor.parse a | refuse .usage "anchor"
    Check.run store (some a)
  | ["export", store, out] => exportVerb store out
  | ["import", store, name, inp, "--digest", d] => importVerb store name inp (.digest d) none
  | ["import", store, name, inp, "--digest", d, "--nonce", n] => importVerb store name inp (.digest d) (some n)
  | ["import", store, name, inp, "--nonce", n, "--digest", d] => importVerb store name inp (.digest d) (some n)
  -- an origin pin needs the invitation's nonce: a fresh one can never make the named origin
  | ["import", store, name, inp, "--origin", o, "--nonce", n] => importVerb store name inp (.origin o) (some n)
  | ["import", store, name, inp, "--nonce", n, "--origin", o] => importVerb store name inp (.origin o) (some n)
# Operating a World

The [RUNBOOK](../deploy/RUNBOOK.md) contains installation/recovery detail. [Host.Cli](../Host/Cli.lean) defines the grammar; [units](../deploy/units/linux) and [exit tables](../deploy/units/EXITS.md) are generated from [Ops.Units](../Ops/Units.lean). Uppercase arguments below stand for operator-supplied values.

| Operation | Form and responsibility |
|---|---|
| Genesis | host genesis STORE NAME [--nonce HEX32] [--certifier HEX32]... [--threshold N] [KEY_HEX NEXT_HEX]... checks founders, authority and budget before creation. Founders independently check the inputs. |
| Writer | host serve STORE [--retain N] serves framed requests. The key-file handle holds the writer lock. Default retention keeps history; an explicit count must meet the floor. |
| Follower | host follow STORE MEMBER_HEX GRANT_HEX owns a separate writable Store and executes member commands. |
| Join | host join STORE INVITE_HEX KEY_HEX [IN] authenticates the invitation under the trusted sponsor key. IN is for a carried invitation. |
| Inspect | host check STORE [--anchor STEPS:CHAIN_HEX] checks history against a held position. The anchor counts steps, not heights. |
| Seal | host seal STORE [--retain N] seals a stopped Store. |
| Carry | host export STORE OUT exports a stopped Store. Host import takes STORE NAME IN with either --digest HEX and optional --nonce HEX32, or --origin HEX with --nonce HEX32. Preserve the checked export pin. |

nd-supervise confines the child and multiplexes bounded socket connections onto framed stdio. Policy grants Store writes and executable access while hiding custody roots. Linux needs the fixed sandbox mechanism and its installation permissions; absence has no permissive fallback.

| Unit | Role |
|---|---|
| nd-host-serve.service | Writer under nd. |
| nd-host-follow.service | Personal follower; its own Store and hidden workspace/holding directories. |
| nd-hosted-follow@.service | Hosted follower under nd-hosted; shared socket group for its driver. |
| nd-follow-drive@.service | nd sync under nd; Restart=always, forty-second interval, except its fatal refusal class. |
| nd-keybroker.service | Provider keys, anchors and pins under nd; confined Lean decider. |
| nd-members@.service | World resident decider and managed follower/member children. |
| nd-grain-launch@.service | Controller and confined grains under nd-hosted. |

nd-members separates member sockets, custody, follower state and follower sockets. Launcher roots must also be disjoint. Ownership, modes and directory chains are checked; a matching path is insufficient. Rendered tmpfiles/sysusers definitions supply identities and modes.

A resident sees its package, writable volume and own member/broker sockets. It reads the Bell, keeps intent before asking, persists the answer before submitting and settles delivery through its member. The broker checks provider policy and custody; the provider secret does not enter the resident volume.

Uncertain is an unknown outcome: use member settlement. Do not invent missing Store files or start a second writer. The [Store layout](../deploy/units/STORE.md) gives backup order: key, atomic base, index, segments. Preserve the backup together and check the restored Store against a held steps/chain anchor. Retention can make older anchors unavailable.

The optional base.cert sidecar is self-authenticating to its consumer, not a replacement for the base/log. [TRUST](TRUST.md) explains the producer boundary.
# Being a member

nd uses ND_WORKSPACE for custody and attempts, and the follower and writer sockets. ND_FOLLOW_SOCK and ND_HOST_SOCK override socket paths. Personal custody uses a separate ND_HOLDING directory for the next secret. Local custody flags are parsed in [nd.rs](../client/src/bin/nd.rs); the shared language is parsed in [Verb](../Protocol/Command/Verb.lean).

These are command forms: uppercase words stand for the member's values. Digests and signed bytes use canonical hex; numbers are decimal. Lang reads package, state and argument text. Describe is spelled read OBJ ?.

| Action and syntax | What is checked |
|---|---|
| Join: nd --join KEY_FILE INVITATION STORE /ABS/HOST | The invitation verifies under the sponsor key obtained through a trusted channel. The joined origin comes from its inputs. Join creates the member's Store and retains the invitation; a sponsor enrolls the join token and supplies a World observe grant. |
| Write: nd write OBJ KEY VALUE [--via GRANT] | After catch-up, follow derives the call against its own World, including pins, authority, laws, bounds and charge. Only an admissible plan is offered for signature. |
| Read: nd read OBJ [KEY=VALUE...] | Follow re-derives history before reading its own state; each supplied expectation must hold. |
| Describe: nd read OBJ ? | Follow loads the package and prints its description, state type, methods, laws and relations. Method indices come from this description. |
| Invoke: nd invoke OBJ METHOD ARGS [--range REL K [PREFIX]] [--pin REL KEY]... [--via GRANT] | Arguments are read at the method's type. Follow plans against authenticated reads and checks the derived turn before requesting its signature. |
| Sync: nd sync | Each event is re-derived; reached steps and chain must match the page. Only read envelopes are signed. |
| Receipt: nd receipt CALL | Follow catches up and builds trie evidence and settlement against its own head. Output includes receipt, head and settlement. |
| Cancel: nd cancel CALL | Follow checks the member's own signed call can be cancelled, requests a cancel signature and sends it. A second catch-up must show the recorded cancellation before success. |

Writes and invokes first sign envelopes for bounded log pages. The eventual call signs its World, member, window, pins and body. A resumed plan is re-derived; changed signed bytes are planMoved. The sponsor key during adoption remains a trust premise described in [TRUST](TRUST.md).

The form nd read OBJ REL K [PREFIX] reads a bounded relation range. The form nd sync --to STEPS:CHAIN compares a position while catching up; --attest signs follow's head, --check ATTEST checks another member's attestation, and --post OBJ posts follow's own head. A follower already beyond the requested comparison answers ahead.

The language also has init, enroll, install, create, law, grant, revoke, rotate, adopt, issue, account, transfer, credit and invite. [Verb.usage](../Protocol/Command/Verb.lean) gives their forms. The --init, --init-hosted, --init-observer, --seal-observer and --keys flags are local custody operations. The --input HEX helper retains founder input; bare invite and adopt KEY_FILE use retained input/invitation files.

A refusal names the failed condition. Stale means a signed pin no longer matches; lawDenied carries the law's reason; noGrant and grantRight concern authority. Parsing and pre-signing refusals create no signed call. An already-sent call needs settlement: uncertain, declined, open and hostContradicted do not mean absent forever. Behind preserves catch-up progress; pageIo identifies a writer page-read failure.

An unsettled attempt stores exact argv, continuation and signed call. Repeating its argv resumes it. The writer reply is a hint; only the follower's receipt settles it. Repeating an already-settled command can create a new call: argv is not a durable caller-intent identifier. Transfer's --once HEX is a separate once-key mechanism.

The [protocol rows](../Protocol/Rows.lean) exercise join, use, describe, receipt, settlement, cancellation and fork comparison through the command machine.
