| 158: “Operational guarantees assume the runbook's filesystem and identity setup; performance limits are judged in the conditions the benchmark names.” | “The runbook specifies required filesystem and identity settings.” “Each benchmark records its measurement conditions.” |
| 163: “These are the lines of work behind the design, and the choices that give nano its particular shape.” | “The design draws on the following work.” |
| 167: “Nano makes the same receiver the source of the writer's Turn and the follower's check, and makes consent before signing a property of that command path.” | “The writer and follower use the same receiver.” “The follower checks a planned call before requesting its signature.” |
| 172: “Nano puts heads and their governing authority into the World's vocabulary, so an object can use a foreign receipt as an input to its own law.” | “Objects can store foreign heads.” “A law can check a transaction receipt against a stored head.” |
| 187: “Its relationship to the reference meaning and the fuel it requires is proved, so ticks have a specified meaning across re-executions.” | “`run_bigStep` relates successful runs to the reference semantics.” “`bigStep_run` proves that a reference result is reachable with sufficient fuel and stack.” |
| 193: “Nano checks that justification against the caller's pinned World and names the failed support when it withdraws a choice; the document makes that behavior visible as a suspended section.” | “Invocation checks each selection's support against pinned observations.” “A refusal identifies failed support.” “The document demonstration displays the resulting suspension.” |
| 207: “Nano makes the verdict readers' ability to recognize failure a standing check, and `Spec/` gives the top claims explicit trivial countermodels.” | “Harness controls test whether verdict readers reject known failures.” “The top claims have trivial countermodels.” |
| 213: “The organizing promise is one lawful history…” | “A member accepts a history checked from its trusted starting state.” State preservation and head comparison separately. |
| 224: “The Bell carries this through a running application…” | “The resident persists request intent before asking the provider.” “It persists the answer before submitting it.” “The Bell rejects a repeated answer to an answered ring.” |
| 232: “Safety is a theorem under stated assumptions; progress is measured on the running system.” | Delete. The named claims and named measurements already say what is established. |
| 298: “nanodregg is rebuilt from minidregg's specification and battleground: its journeys, simulations, workload and meaningful theorem statements.” | “nanodregg draws its requirements from minidregg's scenarios, simulations, workload and theorem statements.” |
| 300: “It is a focused research demonstration that minidregg can draw from, within the Dragon's Egg lineage whose earlier whole-system exploration is dregg (breadstuffs).” | “nanodregg is a research demonstration in the Dragon's Egg project.” “Earlier systems include minidregg and dregg, also called breadstuffs.” |

“The running instance,” “the concrete instance” and “the concrete wiring” repeatedly ask adjectives to explain instantiation.
Name what is bound instead.
`Host.Deployed.X` binds the kernel to the deployed codecs and language.
That sentence needs no second label.

“Start at” is functional navigation when the destination is suitable.
It becomes padding when twelve consecutive paragraphs use it before a source filename.
Put the entry points in a table.
Keep each component's prose for its role and inputs.

